{"id":4197,"date":"2021-11-15T15:40:30","date_gmt":"2021-11-15T23:40:30","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4197"},"modified":"2024-10-29T12:33:38","modified_gmt":"2024-10-29T19:33:38","slug":"1-3-flight-movements-throttle-and-yaw-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-3-flight-movements-throttle-and-yaw-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"1.3 Flight Movements: Throttle and Yaw"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2e70b\" name=\"6a22139b2e70b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2e980\" name=\"6a22139b2e980\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Describe how roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle change the drone\u2019s movement in the air.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use the roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle commands to make the drone navigate a path of your choosing.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Incorporate your knowledge of creating a complete program from Island 1 to build a full flight path for the drone, including takeoff and landing.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this lesson, you will complete the following steps. Make sure that you demonstrate your completed code to your instructor after each step:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Getting Started<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">More Movements: Throttle and Yaw<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Doing the Drone Tango: Putting Movements Together<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Challenge: No One Puts Drone In a Corner<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2ea32\" name=\"6a22139b2ea32\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Started<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Before we learn how drones move, we need to have a program created for testing movement. To do this, follow the steps below:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Make a copy of your <i>pitch.py<\/i> file from the previous lesson and call it <i>yaw.py<\/i><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Change <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">set_pitch()<\/code> to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">set_yaw()<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>Leave all other values the same<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You are now ready to start testing some more movements that your drone can perform!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2ead5\" name=\"6a22139b2ead5\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Yaw<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s left and right rotation. <strong>Yaw<\/strong> is a little different from pitch and roll. While pitch and roll make the drone move left and right or up and down, yaw makes the drone move around its axis. If you remember at the beginning of this lesson, we learned that a drone&#8217;s movement can be tied to the X, Y, and Z axes. So far we have looked at X and Y movement with roll and pitch. Now, we are looking at the Z axis with yaw. With yaw, the drone spins on the Z axis to face a direction. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6146&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Think of yaw as the ability to point the drone in a direction before making it go that way. If someone has ever told you to look at something, you probably rotated your body or head to face that direction. That would be an example of yaw.\u00a0 Yaw makes the drone spin in place to face a direction. Positive yaw will make the CoDrone EDU spin to the left, and negative yaw will make the CoDrone EDU spin to the right.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.set_yaw(power) # power represents power out of 100%\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0# power can be between -100 and 100<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To test out this code, open your <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">yaw.py<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Your code should look like the below:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_yaw(25) # setting the yaw value to 25                        \r\ndrone.move(1)      # drone will yaw for 1 second\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>What did you observe?<\/em> The drone should have spun slowly left<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> for 1 second. Try making the drone spin right and slightly faster for a half second. <em>What does your code look like?<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2eb69\" name=\"6a22139b2eb69\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Throttle<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">As we saw with yaw, the drone can pivot around the Z axis. However, if we want the drone to move up and down the Z axis, we use throttle. Throttle controls the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s vertical, or up and down, movement. Positive throttle will make the CoDrone EDU fly higher, and negative throttle will make the CoDrone EDU fly lower.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6143&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">We have actually used throttle before, possibly without realizing it. <em>Where do you think we have used throttle before?<\/em> If you guessed on takeoff and land, you would be right! The takeoff and land functions use throttle to make the drone rise to a certain height by increasing the throttle and lowering to the ground by decreasing the throttle.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.set_throttle(power) # power represents power out of 100%\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0# power can be between -100 and 100<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To test out this code, make a copy of your <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">yaw.py<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> file and call it <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">throttle.py<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Now change <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">set_pitch()<\/code> to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">set_yaw()<\/code>. You can leave all other values the same. So your code should look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_throttle(25) # setting the throttle value to 25                        \r\ndrone.move(1)      # drone will change throttle for 1 second\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><em>What did you observe? What settings do you think are used for the takeoff and land commands related to throttle?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Be careful with yaw and roll! Yaw will make your CoDrone EDU turn left or right, while roll will move your CoDrone EDU left or right.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2ec15\" name=\"6a22139b2ec15\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Putting Movements Together<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that we know how to use the different movement commands separately, lets see what happens when we use them together! Complete the steps below for our first combined program:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Make a copy of <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">yaw.py<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> or any of your exercise files to this point.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Enter a command to make the drone move forward for 1 second at 20% power<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Enter a command to make the drone move to the left for 2 seconds at 20% power<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you are done, run your code and see what happens! If you are having issues with your code running, here is some example code below:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_pitch(20)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\ndrone.set_roll(-20)\r\ndrone.move(2)\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>What did you observe when you ran the code?<\/em> You may have noticed that the drone moved forward correctly, but then it moved forward and left at the same time. This is because we never set the pitch back to 0. If you remember from earlier, we learned that these <strong>set movement<\/strong> commands were changing the value of a variable. If we don\u2019t set the value back to 0, then it stays whatever value we set it as last. This can actually be valuable if we want to move in two directions at once, like forward and to the right, but was not what we expected.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try to think how you would reset the value of the pitch so that our drone only moves to the left. To do this, we just set the pitch to 0 after the drone has moved forward.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.set_pitch(0)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add this new line of code to your program and test it again. This time the drone should move forward and then left only. Below is what the code should look like with the reset value (minus the importing, takeoff, etc\u2026):<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_pitch(20) # Moves the drone forward at 20% power\r\ndrone.move(1) # Moves for 1 second\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0) # Resets the pitch back to 0\r\ndrone.set_roll(-20) # Moves the drone to the left at 20% power\r\ndrone.move(2) # Moves for 2 seconds\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2ec74\" name=\"6a22139b2ec74\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight Challenges<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Below are a series of challenges to test your flight movement skills! These tasks may require combining movements together simultaneously.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fly in a circle<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6148&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]2. Fly in the shape of a sine wave[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6149&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22139b2ecd8\" name=\"6a22139b2ecd8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about how to fly the drone up and down and turn left and right. Next, you will learn about variables, and how to use them to write better and more efficient code.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-3-flight-movements-throttle-and-yaw-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4734,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2229,2134],"class_list":["post-4197","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-flight-movements","lesson-tag-python","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4197","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":37,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4197\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13078,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4197\/revisions\/13078"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4734"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4197"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4197"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4197"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}